---
layout: application
---
<!--
  Home page. Same shape as the other top-level layouts (about,
  posts_index, publications_index): the h1 and visible lede come
  from the frontmatter of `content/index.md`, and the markdown body
  renders below.

      ---
      title: Hello from Abqari
      description: A short lede shown under the title…
      ---

  Below the markdown body, the layout dispatches a set of optional
  "home modules" — small content cards driven by config:

      home:
        latest_post: true
        random_post: false
        featured_publication: false # showcase-flagged publication, latest_publication design
        latest_publication: true
        next_workshop: false        # soonest upcoming workshop card
        latest_photo: false
        random_photo: false
        photo_gallery: latest       # 'latest' / 'random' / false — single-
                                    #   row gallery of four photos
        contact: false

  Each module renders nothing when the underlying collection is empty
  or its config flag is off. Add featured-content sections (book
  grids, hero treatments, etc.) to `content/index.md` and they'll
  render in the markdown body above the modules.

  The `home__lede` and `home-module*` classes are CSS hooks — see
  `themes/<name>/css/app.css` to style them.
-->
<%
  description = page.frontmatter['description']
  home_cfg    = site.config['home'] || {}

  # Module render order — fixed in the layout; toggle individual
  # modules in config/site.yml under the `home:` block. The first
  # *three* enabled modules receive `priority_image: true` so their
  # hero is fetched eagerly with `fetchpriority="high"` — they're
  # the home page's LCP candidates, and `loading="lazy"` on an
  # above-the-fold image is a known anti-pattern (the browser delays
  # the fetch until viewport visibility is computed, costing 300-500ms
  # on cold loads).
  #
  # Why three: three modules is a realistic above-the-fold count on
  # typical desktop viewports, and the first enabled module isn't
  # guaranteed to actually render an image (e.g. `latest_post`
  # shows the newest post, which may have no `image:` set, in which
  # case the hint is silently wasted). Hinting three covers the
  # fallback chain. Cost when all three render: 2-3 extra eager
  # fetches — cheap insurance for a much faster LCP on the home
  # page's actual image candidate.
  module_order = %w[
    latest_post random_post featured_publication latest_publication
    next_workshop latest_photo random_photo photo_gallery contact
  ]
  priority_modules = module_order.select { |m| home_cfg[m] }.first(3)
%>
<article>
  <header>
    <h1><%= page.title %></h1>
    <% if description && !description.to_s.empty? %>
      <p class="lede"><%= description %></p>
    <% end %>
  </header>
  <%== page.content %>
</article>

<%# Home modules — each is a self-contained section that renders nothing
    when its config flag is false. %>
<% module_order.each do |m| %>
  <% next unless home_cfg[m] %>
  <%== render "partials/home/#{m}", priority_image: priority_modules.include?(m) %>
<% end %>

<%# Schema.org WebSite — the site-wide entity declaration. Identifies
    the publisher (Person or Organization) and links out to all social
    profiles for entity-graph discovery. Rendered on the home page
    only because the entity it describes is the site, not the page. %>
<%== render 'partials/website_jsonld' %>
